{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00434 - MONDO_5709",
  "verbalization": "Cyproheptadine, a drug indicated for urticaria, is linked to the common cold through a chain of indications that involve antihistamine activity. The evidence suggests that Cyproheptadine may exert a pharmacological effect that indirectly influences the common cold, possibly by modulating histamine receptors or other pathways involved in allergic responses. The presence of multiple overlapping indications—urticaria, pheniramine, and alimemazine—indicates a potential mechanism of action that is shared among these antihistamines, suggesting a broader role in respiratory conditions. The integration of these pathways implies that Cyproheptadine may act through a mechanism that affects immune or inflammatory processes, which could contribute to the development or management of the common cold. The evidence supports a plausible but not yet fully elucidated mechanism by which Cyproheptadine may be repurposed for common cold treatment, highlighting the importance of further research into its pharmacological profile.",
